22:18:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You may well have seen a documentary making that claim. The issue is that a documentary’s framing is not, by itself, enough to establish that Eritrean refugees as a group are a growing gang-crime problem across Europe. There have been real, documented violent incidents involving Eritrean diaspora groups in some countries, especially conflicts tied to Eritrean political events and tensions between government supporters and opponents. But that is narrower than “Eritrean gangs” in the usual organized-crime sense, and it should not be generalized to Eritrean refugees or neighborhoods as a whole. A good way to check the claim is to look for: - police or interior-ministry reports naming the specific group and type of offense; - court cases or official assessments showing organized criminal activity rather than isolated incidents; - per-capita or comparable data, not just headline counts; - distinctions between politically motivated clashes, youth disorder, and profit-driven gang crime; - multiple independent sources, including researchers rather than only sensational reporting. If you remember the documentary’s title, country, or examples it cited, I can help assess what its evidence actually supports.
