22:19:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Firsthand experience matters, especially if you’ve seen repeated violence, intimidation, or disorder in a particular area. It can reveal problems that official data or broad national discussions miss. But personal experience also has limits: you may see the most visible or disruptive subset of people, while many other Eritrean residents are simply working, studying, and living quietly. That does not make your observations fake; it means they should be framed as evidence about a place, situation, or network—not a verdict about everyone sharing an origin. A practical way to put it is: - “There have been recurring problems around these streets/events/groups, and I want authorities to deal with them.” - Not: “People from Eritrea are inherently likely to be gang members.” Those are importantly different claims. The first supports concrete action—reporting crime, demanding effective policing, safer public spaces, youth services, and accountability for organizers of violent events—without assuming that a neighbor or traveler is dangerous because of nationality.
